'''2.''' Without knowledge of art; ignorant of the fine arts. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|And as for Mr. Ruskin’s world of art being a place where he may take life easily, woe to the luckless mortal who enters it with any such disposition. . . . The poor wanderer soon begins to look back with infinite longing to the lost paradise of the ''artless''.
{{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Portraits of Places, p. 68.}}}}
'''3.''' Showing no artistic skill; inartistic; rude. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Had it been a practice of the Saxons to set up these assemblages of ''artless'' and massy pillars, more specimens would have remained. {{right|''T. Warton'', Hist. of Kiddington.}}}}
'''4.''' Free from guile, craft, or stratagem; simple; sincere; unaffected; undesigning; unsophisticated: as, an ''artless'' mind.
{{smaller block|The little ''artless'' Rosey warbled on her pretty ditties.
{{right|''Thackeray'', The Newcomes, II. 58.}}}}
='''Syn. 4.''' {{smaller|Guileless, open, candid, frank, natural, unaffected, ingenuous, simple-minded, naïve, honest.}}

'''artlessly''' (ärt′les-li), ''adv.'' In an artless manner. (''a'') Without art or skill. [Rare.] (''b'') Without guile; naturally; sincerely; unaffectedly.
{{smaller block|<poem>Some buds she arranged with a vast deal of care,
To look as if ''artlessly'' twined in her hair.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Fable for Critics.}}}}

'''artlessness''' (ärt′les-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being artless; simplicity; sincerity; unaffectedness.

'''artly†''' (ärt′li), ''a.'' [< ''art''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Artistic; skilful; artful. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Their ''artly'' and pleasing relation.
{{right|''Chapman'', Odyssey, ix. 212.}}}}

